smoltcp: several NICs sharing the sockets, each with its own lease

netd drives the virtio NIC and the virtual NICs of /dev/tap (the Wi-Fi
link) over one socket set, each NIC with a lease of its own. Upstream, an
interface sends whatever it can route, whatever the source address, so a
connection made on one link would leave through the other; every DHCP
reply goes to the first DHCP socket of the set; and a NIC's interface
would send another NIC's address to the router instead of leaving it to
the loopback interface.

set_own_source_only() makes an interface send only the packets whose
source is one of its addresses (or unspecified: a DHCP request), leaving
the others in their socket for the NIC they belong to (as local-pair
does); set_elsewhere_addrs() names the other NICs' addresses, which a
set_local_elsewhere() interface leaves to the loopback one; and
Dhcpv4Socket::set_owner() ties a DHCP socket to the NIC with that
hardware address, for its requests and its replies. An ICMP socket's
source is the interface's own address, so the first NIC to poll it sent
it: IcmpSocket::set_source() names it, and with it the NIC.

